 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** Adds the forward and backward pass of the discriminator network to each training step [S1], [S3]. The discriminator architecture typically uses 14 convolutional layers with $4 \times 4$ filters and LReLU activations [S3].
-*   **Performance:** Improves perceptual quality and high-frequency details compared to MSE/L1-only training [S1], [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces sharper, more realistic textures; reduces smearing and streak artifacts common in single-view reconstruction [S2], [S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful hyperparameter tuning for loss weights; necessitates per-reconstruction discriminator retraining [S3].
-*   **When to use:** When photorealism and high-frequency detail are prioritized over strict pixel-wise accuracy [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is slower than supervised methods due to the alternating optimization of two networks (generator and discriminator) [S1, S2]. The multi-scale discriminator adds computational overhead proportional to $N_s$ [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires memory for both generator and discriminator networks. The discriminator is removed at test time, so inference memory matches the generator alone [S2].
+*   **Performance:** Produces more realistic textures and fewer artifacts than pure L1/L2 loss methods, particularly in high-frequency details [S2]. FID scores are commonly used to evaluate quality [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves generalization on unseen inputs and handles insufficient point cloud information better than non-adversarial methods [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Training instability is a risk; requires careful tuning of hyperparameters like $\lambda$ and learning rates [S3]. Not given in the sources: specific hardware scaling factors.
 
 ## Background
-Originated from Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) introduced by Goodfellow et al. [S1]. In volumetric rendering and super-resolution, it was first employed to train a discriminator network in parallel with a super-resolution generator to classify images as ground truth or not, using the discriminator's output as a loss term [S1]. The methodology was subsequently adapted for point-based graphics with multi-scale discriminators [S2], fluid reconstruction with fully convolutional discriminators [S3], and hierarchical 3D Gaussian splats with pose-aware contrastive objectives [S4].
+Adversarial training in volume rendering and novel view synthesis was introduced to address the blurriness and artifacts common in supervised learning approaches [S1, S2]. The method draws from Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) proposed by Goodfellow et al. [S1]. It has been applied to super-resolution of volumetric data [S1], point-based graphics [S2], and 3D Gaussian splatting [S3].
 
